          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em Kan complex
            is an abstraction of the combinatorial structure found in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+simplicial+complex"} singular simplicial complex
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retractions"} retractions
            of any geometric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplex
            to any of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horns"} horns
            – simplices missing one face and their interior – means that all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horns"} horns
            in the singular simplicial complex can be filled with genuine simplices. This is the
            = succeed "." do
              %em Kan filler condition
          %p
            At the same time, a Kan complex is an abstraction of the structure found in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Duskin+nerve"} Duskin nerve
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-groupoid"} 2-groupoid
            and generally (almost by definition, see at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-groupoids"} n-groupoids
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \leq \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+definition+of+higher+category"} geometric model
            for
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            which is based on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+shapes+for+higher+structures"} shape
            given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
            (as opposed to, say, the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globe+category"} globe category
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %p
            In more detail, a Kan complex is a collection of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-shaped" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplex
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-morphism"} k-morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
            = succeed "-morphisms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-morphisms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            Specifically for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒢
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(\mathcal{G}_\bullet)
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_\bullet
            = succeed "-cell" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi …
                    %mo →
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0\to 1\to \ldots \to k\}
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplex
            by taking its
            = succeed "-faces" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k-1)
          %p
            Hence, in a Kan complex, a
            = succeed "-face" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k+1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplex
            may be thought of as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            of the remaining faces, all regarded as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-morphisms"} k-morphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            (a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-groupoid"} 1-groupoid
            %em choosing
            one of the fillers of each horn in a Kan complex to be
            %em the
            composite means passing from Kan complexes to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+definition+of+higher+category"} algebraic model
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+Kan+complexes"} algebraic Kan complexes
          %p
            Among all
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} strict ∞-groupoids
            correspond to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+complexes"} crossed complexes
            and various other related algebraic models, all or most of which have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} faithful embedding
            into Kan complexes under a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            operation. One of these are the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+T-complexes"} simplicial T-complexes
          %h2#definition Definition
          #KanComplexes.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em Kan complex
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              that satisfies the
              = succeed "," do
                %em Kan condition
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  all
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horns"} horns
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
