          %p
            (even unique fillers, due to the properties of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            of an ordinary category).
          %p
            This is one way to see and motivate that a simplicial set that is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            but which does not necessarily have unique fillers models an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
          %p Accordingly
          .un_prop
            %h6#proposition_5 Proposition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(C)
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-category"} strict ∞-category
              is a Kan complex if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} strict ∞-groupoid
          %h3#kan_complexes_from_simplicial_groups Kan complexes from simplicial groups
          %p
            The underlying simplicial set of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} simplicial group
            (see there) is a Kan complex.
          %p
            In particular, via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            (see there)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                  %munderover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi DK
                  %mi sAb
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi forget
                  %mi KanCplx
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi sSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Ch_{\bullet \geq 0}
                  \\underoverset{\simeq}{DK}{\to}
                  sAb
                  \\stackrel{forget}{\to}
                  KanCplx
                  \\hookrightarrow
                  sSet
          %p
            every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            (of abelian groups, in non-negative degree) is equivalent to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+abelian+group"} simplicial abelian group
            and this has an underlying Kan complex. This way,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            becomes a special case of the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            (of Kan complexes).
          %h3#simplicial_models_of_malcev_theories Simplicial models of Mal’cev theories
          %p
            Generalizing the example of simplicial groups: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mal%27cev+theory"} Mal'cev theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            which admits a Mal’cev operation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product-preserving+functor"} product-preserving functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A: T \to Set^{\Delta^{op}}
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/12/tr12.pdf#270"} Source
          %p
            For example, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            in simplicial sets is a Kan complex because it is an internal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
          %p
            It is not hard to imagine how the proof might go, by adapting the proof in the special case of simplicial groups, replacing occurrences of elements of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %msup
                    %mi y
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x y^{-1} z
            by values
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t(x, y, z)
            of a chosen Mal’cev operation.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_9 Remark
            %p
              Somewhat more generally, for any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mal%27cev+category"} Mal'cev category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^{op} \to C
              satisfies the Kan condition. This was shown by
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#CKP"} Carboni, Kelly, and Pedicchio
          %h3#singular_simplicial_complexes__fundamental_groupoids
            Singular simplicial complexes / fundamental
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+simplicial+complex"} singular simplicial complex
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(X)
            (often denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(X)
            or
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sing
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sing(X)
            = succeed "-simplices" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi Top
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi Top
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Pi(X)_n := Top(\Delta^n_{Top}, X)
          %p
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            of continuous maps from the standard topological
            = succeed "-simplex" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi Top
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n_{Top}
            into
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Using the fact that the
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi Top
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n_{Top}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} cosimplicial space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi Top
                  %mo :
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta_{Top} : \Delta \to Top
          %p
            in the obvious way, the
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(X)_n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            in the corresponding obvious way. For instance, the face maps are induced by restricting maps to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            along the face inclusions
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi δ
                      %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta^i : \Delta^{n-1} \hookrightarrow \Delta^n
          %p
            That
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(X)
            is indeed a Kan complex is intuitively clear. Technically, it follows from the fact that the inclusions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Λ
                            %mi n
                        %mi Top
                    %mi k
                  %mo ↪
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi Top
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {{\Lambda^n}_{Top}}_k \hookrightarrow \Delta^n_{Top}
            of topological horns into topological simplices are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retracts"} retracts
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi Top
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Λ
                            %mi n
                        %mi Top
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n_{Top} \to {{\Lambda^n}_{Top}}_k
            given by “squashing” a topological
            = succeed "-simplex" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Λ
                            %mi n
                        %mi Top
                    %mi k
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi Top
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Λ
                            %mi n
                        %mi Top
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Id
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  ({{\Lambda^n}_{Top}}_k \to \Delta^n_{Top} \to
                  {{\Lambda^n}_{Top}}_k)
                  \=
                  Id
                  \\,.
          %p
            Therefore, the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mi k
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Delta^n, \Pi(X)] \to [\Lambda^n_k,\Pi(X)]
            is an epimorphism, since it is equal to the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mi k
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top(\Delta^n, X) \to Top(\Lambda^n_k, X)
            which has a right inverse
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Λ
                      %mi k
                      %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Top
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top(\Lambda^n_k, X) \to Top(\Delta^n, X)
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            represented by the Kan complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(X)
            is called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} fundamental ∞-groupoid
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            This example is the universal one: up to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} weak equivalence of Kan complexes
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
          %p
            This is the statement of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
            (which is a theorem for
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %h2#cubical_analogue Cubical analogue
          %p
            The simplicial notion of a Kan complex has an analogue for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+set"} cubical sets
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+Kan+complex"} cubical Kan complex
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              A slight weakening of the Kan condition, the
              %em weak Kan condition
              leads to the definition of
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
          %p Other concepts:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg+subcomplex"} Eilenberg subcomplex
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+Kan+complex"} weak Kan complex
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+objects"} Kan objects
            can be defined in any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
          %p see there for more information.
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Originally, Kan complexes were defined in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+set"} cubical setting
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+M.+Kan"}> Daniel M. Kan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Abstract homotopy. I
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1073/pnas.41.12.1092"} doi
          %p The second paper in the series proves that the cubical singular complex functor yields cubical Kan complexes:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+M.+Kan"}> Daniel M. Kan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Abstract homotopy. II
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1073/pnas.42.5.255"} doi
          %p
            The third paper introduces simplicial Kan complexes (as “c.s.s. complexes which satisfy the extension condition”), and defines and studies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibrant+replacement"} Kan fibrant replacement
            functor:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+M.+Kan"}> Daniel M. Kan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Abstract homotopy. III
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1073/pnas.42.7.419"} doi
          %p
            The fourth paper proves that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+groups"} simplicial groups
            are Kan complexes and studies their properties, including the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+loop+group"} Kan loop group
            functor:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+M.+Kan"}> Daniel M. Kan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Abstract homotopy. IV
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1073/pnas.42.8.542"} doi
          %p The details for Part III above appeared in the paper
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+M.+Kan"}> Daniel M. Kan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On c.s.s. complexes
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.2307/2372558"} doi
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+C.+Moore"} John C. Moore
            in his review of this paper for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mathematical+Reviews"} Mathematical Reviews
            indicates that the term “Kan complex” was already in use at the time:
          %blockquote
            %p For such complexes, usually called Kan complexes, homotopy groups may be defined, and, further, the notion of homotopy between maps of an arbitrary semi-simplicial complex into a Kan complex is an equivalence relation.
          %p The details for Part IV above are given in the following paper:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+M.+Kan"}> Daniel M. Kan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Combinatorial Definition of Homotopy Groups
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.2307/1970006"} doi
          %p Exposition:
          %ul
            %li#Friedman08
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Greg+Friedman"}> Greg Friedman
              , Section 7 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em An elementary illustrated introduction to simplicial sets
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0809.4221"} arXiv:0809.4221
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/journals/rocky-mountain-journal-of-mathematics/volume-42/issue-2/Survey-Article-An-elementary-illustrated-introduction-to-simplicial-sets/10.1216/RMJ-2012-42-2-353.full"} doi:10.1216/RMJ-2012-42-2-353
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li#GabrielZisman67
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Gabriel"}> Pierre Gabriel
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michel+Zisman"} Michel Zisman
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calculus+of+fractions+and+homotopy+theory"} Calculus of fractions and homotopy theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.math.rochester.edu/people/faculty/doug/otherpapers/GZ.pdf"} pdf
            %li#GoerssJardine09
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Goerss"}> Paul Goerss
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+F.+Jardine"} J. F. Jardine
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simplicial+homotopy+theory"} Simplicial homotopy theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-0346-0189-4"} doi:10.1007/978-3-0346-0189-4
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://web.archive.org/web/19990208220238/http://www.math.uwo.ca/~jardine/papers/simp-sets/"} webpage
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kan_fibration"} Kan fibration
          %p
            For Kan complexes as such, see also the references at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} classical model structure on simplicial sets
          %p
            For Kan complexes as
            = succeed "-groupoids," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kerodon"}> Kerodon
                ,
                %em Kan complexes
                (
                %a{:href => "https://kerodon.net/tag/00SY"}> 00SY
                )
          %p An early mention of this idea was in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Porter"}> Tim Porter
              , Letter to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/16-06-1983.pdf", :title => ""} pdf
          %p
            For background on the general relation of simplicial- and globular sets, see also the references at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oriental"} oriental
          %p
            Discussion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} smooth ∞-groupoids
            presented by “Lie-Kan complexes” is in section 4.2 of
          %ul#CKP
            %li
              %p#NSSb
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Nikolaus"}> Thomas Nikolaus
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Danny+Stevenson"} Danny Stevenson
                %em
                  Principal
                  = succeed "-bundles" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1207.0249"}> arXiv:1207.0249
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aurelio+Carboni"}> A. Carboni
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Kelly"} G.M. Kelly
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Some remarks on Maltsev and Goursat categories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://link.springer.com/article/10.1007%2FBF00872942"} Springer link
        .revisedby
